facts:
A **probability histogram** draws each $P(x)$ as a bar of that height above its value of $x$. For consecutive integer values, the bars have unit width and touch.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.8cm,every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.8,0);
\foreach \y/\lbl in {1/0.1,2/0.2,3/0.3,4/0.4} {\draw (-0.1,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\lbl$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.5,0) rectangle (1.5,2);
\draw (1.5,0) rectangle (2.5,3);
\draw (2.5,0) rectangle (3.5,4);
\draw (3.5,0) rectangle (4.5,1);
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4} {\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,0) {$\x$};}
\node[rotate=90] at (-0.8,2) {$P(X=x)$};
\node[below] at (2.4,-1.0) {Value of $X$};
\node at (2.4,5.1) {Probability histogram};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
$0.15+0.4+0.3+0.15=1$, so the heights of the four bars must add to $1$.
Draw a unit-width bar of height $P(x)$ above each value of $x$, with adjacent bars touching.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.8cm,every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.8,0);
\foreach \y/\lbl in {1/0.1,2/0.2,3/0.3,4/0.4} {\draw (-0.1,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,\y) {$\lbl$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.1,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.5,0) rectangle (1.5,1.5);
\draw (1.5,0) rectangle (2.5,4);
\draw (2.5,0) rectangle (3.5,3);
\draw (3.5,0) rectangle (4.5,1.5);
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4} {\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,0) {$\x$};}
\node[rotate=90] at (-0.8,2) {$P(X=x)$};
\node[below] at (2.4,-1.0) {Books borrowed};
\node at (2.4,5.1) {Books borrowed in a day};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
